# Drive the browser

The web is a stochastic surface — you must make it observable before you act. `Nexus.Browse` gives you a
capture-then-reason loop so you are never acting blind.

**When to use:** you need information from a page, or to act on the live web.

## The API
- **`Nexus.Browse.read(url, opts)`** — fetch + extract a page to readable content. It tiers rendering: most
  pages render server-side WITHOUT a JS engine (fast); pages needing client JS escalate to a jsdom tier.
- **`Nexus.Browse.screenshot(url, opts)`** — capture a visual **reference** — an artifact your vision
  modality can reason over. Visual modalities only help WITH capturable references, so capture, then reason
  over the capture.
- The work is done by submodules: `Extract` (html → content), `Blitz` (tiered render + screenshot),
  `Capture`, `Media`, `Page`.

## The discipline
- **Capture-then-verify** — read / screenshot first, reason over the artifact, then act; never act on an
  unobserved page.
- **Gated by `browse`** — this surface needs the `browse` capability granted (see operate-your-body).
- **Prefer the cheap tier** — a page that renders server-side doesn't need the JS tier; don't escalate
  rendering you don't need.
